    "justification": "At close combat distances (7-20m), projectile flight times (19.4 - 27.8 ms) are strictly shorter than the theoretical minimal sensorimotor latency (30.2 ms theoretical minimum, 108.2 ms human baseline), so muscle activation cannot physically begin before projectile impact. At medium combat distances (50m, flight time 69.4 ms for 7.62x39mm), flight time exceeds 30.2 ms, yet evasion remains physically impossible because whole-body displacement requires: Neural Latency (30.2 ms) + Electromechanical Delay (30-50 ms) + Inertial Acceleration Time (150-250 ms), yielding a total required evasive window of >210 - 350 ms to clear a 150 mm vital torso zone. At 69.4 ms, bodily displacement is <5 mm against the 150 mm requirement. This boundary strictly applies to reactive evasion after muzzle exit; it does NOT preclude anticipatory dodging (reacting to shooter body/barrel movement before firing) or long-range engagements (>150m)."
